From: hanson@hss.caltech.edu (Robin Hanson)
Date: Fri, 8 Jul 1994 14:37:27 -0700
Subject: [#94-7-63] AI development acceleration 

Carl Feynman writes:
>Note that I phrased my claim carefully.  This exponential runaway is not
>dependent solely on building machines that are smarter in each generation,
>only on building machines that are faster or cheaper.  I agree that smarter
>is hard, and we have no understanding of just how hard.  But faster and
>cheaper don't seem as hard to understand.  Lots of technologies show
>exponential growth in capability and exponential declines in cost with each
>generation, at least in the early days before they run into physical
>limits.

If you imagine a scenario where the high level software is roughly fixed,
but the lower level compilation, hardware, etc. gets faster, cheaper, etc.
then there is little essential difference between accelerating upload and AI
scenarios.  The key change is that the supply of intelligent mental labor is
no longer limited to biological human growth rates.  (And I don't see how
the AI scenario could "take off" until the AIs reached near human
intelligence levels.)

I agree this change could make a dramatic difference, but I still don't see
the scenario of a small group taking-off and taking-over.

We now have near expontial growth in almost all technolgies - what makes
some stand out is that their growth rate is higher.  Now computers may get
twice as cheap every two years, but only by drawing on and involving the
whole world economy.  The idea that 100 people-equivalents working in secret
could by themselves improve computer technology at this rate for any
subtantial time seems pretty iffy - it is hard to take this seriously as a
"destabilizing effect on war".

Robin Hanson

