I've heard the claim before that "putrefaction or fermentation" in your
digestive system is bad for you, and as it is here, the claim is not backed
up.  I guess the idea of food putrefying inside you is so disgusting that
it's just obviously got to bad for you, requiring no justification.  But I
thought that it was well known that bacteria in the intestines do a lot of
useful work in digesting stuff down into molecules we can absorb.  So it
sounds like putrefaction and fermentation are to be encouraged.  Certainly
my experiences with oral antibiotics that sterilize the digestive tract
have not been happy ones.

>
>They cite the work of Arthur Cason, M.D., who "...reported in
>1945 that he and his aides conducted 2 groups of experiments
>that showed that eating protein and carbohydrates at the same
>meal retards and even prevents digestion. He made control tests
>in which digestive rates were recorded and a final analysis of the
>feces was made. He said, "Such tests always reveal that the digestion
>of proteins when mixed with starches is retarded in the stomach;
>the degree varying in different individuals, and also in the
>particular protein or starch ingested."..If food is properly
>combined, it is fully broken down, absorbed, and utilized by the
>body."(Fit For Life, pg 52)

If they can't find a reference more recent than 1945, that probably means
it hasn't been replicated, which probably means it can't be, since it's an
interesting result and people probably tried.  Most interesting results
from 1945 (in sciences I know well) have either been replicated a bunch of
times and are now in textbooks, or failed to be replicated and are now
forgotten.  And it's from a non-peer-reviewed publication?  Well, I
wouldn't change my eating habits based on it.

Here's another unspoken assumption in the above: that it is important that
food be "fully utilized".  Why do you care if some fraction of the
nutrients in your food are going right through you and down the drain?
Just eat a little more of the same and you'll be fine.  And your septic
system will thank you for it.

I have a mild and intermittent case of ulcerative colitis, a chronic
disease which, among other things, speeds up peristalsis.  When I'm having
an attack, food goes through me somehat faster than usual.  I find that
this increases my appetite.  It's kind of fun-- I can eat big hearty meals
at every sitting and not grow fat.  I have to watch that I don't fall
behind and start losing weight.

>Comments, factoids, opinions appreciated. Thanks.

Reliability rating for this post: I have never made an effort to learn
about nutrition (except as it relates to colitis) so this post is pretty
much just opinionated sounding-off.


