From: carlf@media.mit.edu (Carl Feynman)
Date: Sun, 10 Jul 1994 02:46:46 -0100
Subject: [#94-7-73] AI development acceleration

Robin Hanson writes:
>If you imagine a scenario where the high level software is roughly fixed,
>but the lower level compilation, hardware, etc. gets faster, cheaper, etc.
>then there is little essential difference between accelerating upload and AI
>scenarios.

I have been assuming (without saying so) quite the opposite scenraio: the
hardware is fixed, but the software keeps getting improved by substantial
factors.  I think the extraordinary rates of progress in hardware, versus
the minuscule rates of progress in AI (or uploading), will produce quite an
overhang by the time we finally get an intelligent machine.

In such a scenario, an AI would be different from an upload in that it
would probably be easier to redesign and improve an AI without breaking it,
since we knew how to design it in the first place.

>  (And I don't see how
>the AI scenario could "take off" until the AIs reached near human
>intelligence levels.)

Of course.  At least near-human intelligence at engineering tasks, anyway.

>
>I agree this change could make a dramatic difference, but I still don't see
>the scenario of a small group taking-off and taking-over.
>
>We now have near expontial growth in almost all technolgies - what makes
>some stand out is that their growth rate is higher.  Now computers may get
>twice as cheap every two years, but only by drawing on and involving the
>whole world economy.  The idea that 100 people-equivalents working in secret
>could by themselves improve computer technology at this rate for any
>subtantial time seems pretty iffy

How fast a small number of people can advance a technology depends on how
well-developed the technology is.  In the beginning, there is a lot of
"low-hanging fruit"-- you just walk around plucking a 10% improvement here
and a 20% improvement there.  Eventually it gets harder, and you need big
research and development teams with lots of specialists and subcontractors.
Look at airplanes before 1910 or computers before 1952: radical
improvements were made by tiny teams.  As the technology matures, you can
maintain exponential improvement, but only by throwing huge development
effort at it.  This is where airplanes were up until about 1970, and
computers are today.

I was implicitly assuming that there were six orders of magnitude available
in the "low-hanging fruit" stage of development.  I guess you're right that
that seems implausible.  I wouldn't be suprised if there were three orders
of magnitude, though.

> - it is hard to take this seriously as a
>"destabilizing effect on war".

I agree.  I got started on this topic not because I was claiming that AI
would lead to war, but because someone said that an AI runaway was
impossible, based on analogy to existing technology.


--carlf

